Below is a concise, JSON‑formatted list of the long‑weekend holidays that were observed in Canada during 2023.  
Each entry shows the **date** of the holiday, its **name**, and the **province(s)** where it was a statutory holiday.  

```json
[
  {
    "date": "2023-02-20",
    "holiday": "Family Day",
    "provinces": [
      "Alberta",
      "British Columbia",
      "Manitoba",
      "New Brunswick",
      "Ontario",
      "Saskatchewan"
    ]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-03-31",
    "holiday": "Good Friday",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-05-22",
    "holiday": "Victoria Day",
    "provinces": ["All provinces except Quebec"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-07-01",
    "holiday": "Canada Day",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-09-04",
    "holiday": "Labour Day",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-10-09",
    "holiday": "Thanksgiving",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-11-11",
    "holiday": "Remembrance Day",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  },
  {
    "date": "2023-12-25",
    "holiday": "Christmas Day",
    "provinces": ["All provinces"]
  }
]
```

### How to use this data

* **Date** – The actual day the holiday falls on.  
  *If the holiday falls on a weekend, the statutory holiday is usually observed on the following Monday (e.g., Canada Day on July 1, 2023 was observed on Monday July 3).*

* **Holiday** – The name of the holiday.  

* **Provinces** – Which provinces observe the holiday.  
  *“All provinces” means the holiday is a statutory holiday across the entire country.*  

Feel free to import this JSON directly into your holiday‑planning app or adapt it to your own data schema. If you need more granular details (e.g., substitute days, regional variations, or additional provincial holidays), let me know!
